Z||000025||falconhulk||12-31-2001||09:09 AM||||I dont think so FieroLisa.  Every long term relationship goes through periods where the other person irritates you.  Our society has changed woman for the worse.  Woman are much more likely today to walk out of ANY relationship then they were 30 years ago.  They are also much more likely to have a mental illness.  Our society is really screwing woman up.  The worst part of that is that woman are the backbone of our families and therefore our world.  It seems like woman can be influenced by anything they see or hear on TV now.  Look at that Lifetime network.  Its a "Womans" network but all they play are movies about domestic abuse and rape.  You may say this is just TV but it seems that most woman try to out do each other for the worst life experiance.  It use to be woman pushed the man higher so that thier collective lives were better.  It just does not make sense anymore.  I will probably get flamed for this but I think its mens fault.  We have hancuffed ourselves today so that we are not the men they grew up dreaming of.  We are the products of scholl teachers and television values.  This society is tearing itself apart.||||reg||1
Z||000026||FieroLisa||12-31-2001||04:02 PM||||falcon- of course any and every relationshipo goes through phases where the man and woman irritate each other. But the whole idea is to find the person whose irritations are one that you can live with, maybe even enjoy. <BR>There have been guys I've dated that may do something that irritates me, however little it may be. If this guy isn't the "right guy" at some point this quirk is going to drive us to break up. Someone else may do this same thing, but if I feel differently about this person, love them, and want to spend my life with them, that quirk will be more insignifigant. <BR>The decline of women? Well, 50 years ago there was the unwritten rule that women were just supposed to take care of the kids, clean the house, and wait patiently for their husbands to get home and give them foot rubs.  Will that work in today's society? Maybe for SOME women, but I am a competitive, career-driven woman and if that means I wasn't married at 18 cooking dinner in a pink checkered ruffled apron, that's perfectly fine with me. <BR>||||reg||1
